  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap integral C\u2085 for a step function h with given constraints. Current methods use differential evolution, which may struggle with local optima due to non-convexity. The proposed approach employs an adaptive gradient descent with constraints handling, leveraging problem structure for efficiency.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nGiven h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1], discretized into n_points, the objective is:\n\n$$C_5 = \\max_k \\sum_{x=0}^{2 - |k|} h(x)(1 - h(x+k))$$\n\nsubject to 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h = n_points / 2.\n\nThe gradient \u2207C\u2085 is computed for the dominant lag k*, guiding h updates.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize h with initial_h_values, normalized to meet sum constraint.\nb. Compute cross-correlations for all lags to find current C\u2085.\nc. For each iteration:\n   i. Compute cross-correlations and identify k*.\n   ii. Compute gradient contributions from k*.\n   iii. Update h with learning rate \u03b7: h = h - \u03b7\u2207C\u2085.\n   iv. Project h onto [0,1] and rescale to maintain sum constraint.\n   v. Adjust \u03b7 based on improvement; perturb h if stagnation detected.\nd. Terminate when budget exhausted; return best h.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Start with \u03b7 = 0.1, decay by 0.95 every 50 steps.\n- Perturb h by \u00b10.01 after 100 steps with no improvement.\n- Use n_points=70 for balance between accuracy and speed.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEach iteration: O(n_points^2) for cross-correlation, with up to 1000 iterations. With n=70, ~5e4 operations per second on CPU, fits within 1100s, allowing ~2000 iterations.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure h remains within [0,1] and sum(h) exact. Validate final C\u2085 via evaluator and return h with its C\u2085.",
